1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,708
Six trials.

2
00:00:00,768 --> 00:00:02,494
Four death sentences.

3
00:00:02,554 --> 00:00:04,187
Twenty-three years.

4
00:00:04,247 --> 00:00:05,369
One man.

5
00:00:05,429 --> 00:00:07,648
Most Americans believe there is a wall in the law

6
00:00:07,649 --> 00:00:10,148
that says the government cannot keep trying you

7
00:00:10,149 --> 00:00:12,128
for the same crime until it gets

8
00:00:12,129 --> 00:00:13,564
the answer it wants.

9
00:00:13,624 --> 00:00:15,582
That wall has a gap in it.

10
00:00:15,642 --> 00:00:17,301
If your conviction is thrown out,

11
00:00:17,302 --> 00:00:19,242
or your jury can't agree,

12
00:00:19,302 --> 00:00:21,594
the state is allowed to start over.

13
00:00:21,654 --> 00:00:23,654
In a small town in Mississippi,

14
00:00:23,714 --> 00:00:26,844
one prosecutor drove through that gap six times —

15
00:00:26,904 --> 00:00:29,853
against the same man, for the same four murders,

16
00:00:29,854 --> 00:00:31,879
across twenty-three years.

17
00:00:31,939 --> 00:00:34,826
Every one of those death sentences was erased.

18
00:00:34,886 --> 00:00:37,705
And when the Supreme Court
of the United States finally read the record

19
00:00:37,706 --> 00:00:39,146
of what he had done,

20
00:00:39,206 --> 00:00:42,026
it needed only one adjective at a time:

21
00:00:42,086 --> 00:00:43,207
relentless.

22
00:00:43,267 --> 00:00:44,575
Determined.

23
00:00:44,635 --> 00:00:46,614
This is the story of the six trials

24
00:00:46,615 --> 00:00:47,634
of Curtis Flowers —

25
00:00:47,635 --> 00:00:51,948
and of the man who would not stop.

26
00:00:52,008 --> 00:00:54,013
His name is Curtis Flowers.

27
00:00:54,073 --> 00:00:57,573
In the summer of 1996 he was twenty-six years old,

28
00:00:57,633 --> 00:00:59,513
living in Winona, Mississippi —

29
00:00:59,573 --> 00:01:01,933
a gospel singer in his family's group,

30
00:01:01,993 --> 00:01:04,948
a young man with no criminal record of any kind.

31
00:01:05,008 --> 00:01:07,147
By the winter he was in a cell, charged

32
00:01:07,148 --> 00:01:09,985
with the worst crime his town had ever seen.

33
00:01:10,045 --> 00:01:12,375
What happened next is not a whodunit.

34
00:01:12,435 --> 00:01:14,114
Nobody has ever proven who committed

35
00:01:14,115 --> 00:01:15,515
these murders —

36
00:01:15,575 --> 00:01:16,254
remember that,

37
00:01:16,255 --> 00:01:19,734
because this story refuses to pretend otherwise.

38
00:01:19,794 --> 00:01:21,893
This is a different kind of mystery:

39
00:01:21,894 --> 00:01:23,853
how one elected official was able to try

40
00:01:23,854 --> 00:01:27,754
the same man six times, lose four convictions,

41
00:01:27,814 --> 00:01:30,594
burn through two juries, and keep going —

42
00:01:30,654 --> 00:01:33,269
and what it finally took to stop him.

43
00:01:33,329 --> 00:01:35,068
Here is how to watch this film:

44
00:01:35,069 --> 00:01:36,402
count everything.

45
00:01:36,462 --> 00:01:37,956
Count the trials.

46
00:01:38,016 --> 00:01:39,789
Count the death sentences.

47
00:01:39,849 --> 00:01:41,189
Count the jurors —

48
00:01:41,249 --> 00:01:43,618
who they were, and who was removed.

49
00:01:43,678 --> 00:01:44,777
Because in the end,

50
00:01:44,778 --> 00:01:46,557
this case wasn't decided by a confession

51
00:01:46,558 --> 00:01:47,912
or a fingerprint.

52
00:01:47,972 --> 00:01:50,116
It was decided by arithmetic.

53
00:01:50,176 --> 00:01:52,134
The numbers are going to testify.

54
00:01:52,194 --> 00:01:53,673
And the last number will arrive

55
00:01:53,674 --> 00:01:57,835
from the highest courtroom in the country.

56
00:01:57,895 --> 00:01:58,834
Start with the morning,

57
00:01:58,835 --> 00:02:00,994
because four people deserve to be more

58
00:02:00,995 --> 00:02:02,454
than a case number.

59
00:02:02,514 --> 00:02:04,797
July 16th, 1996.

60
00:02:04,857 --> 00:02:06,437
Winona, Mississippi —

61
00:02:06,497 --> 00:02:09,637
a town of roughly five thousand people,

62
00:02:09,697 --> 00:02:12,657
about half of them Black and half of them white,

63
00:02:12,717 --> 00:02:15,267
sitting at the crossroads of the state.

64
00:02:15,327 --> 00:02:18,447
On Front Street, downtown, stood Tardy Furniture,

65
00:02:18,507 --> 00:02:20,326
a family store that had been part of the town

66
00:02:20,327 --> 00:02:21,744
for generations.

67
00:02:21,804 --> 00:02:24,180
That morning, four people were inside.

68
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:26,940
Bertha Tardy, fifty-nine, the owner —

69
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,839
she had run that store with her late husband

70
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:30,749
and kept it going after him.

71
00:02:30,809 --> 00:02:33,650
Carmen Rigby, forty-five, who kept the books.

72
00:02:33,710 --> 00:02:36,736
Robert Golden, forty-two, who worked the floor.

73
00:02:36,796 --> 00:02:38,276
And Derrick Stewart —

74
00:02:38,336 --> 00:02:40,096
everyone called him Bobo —

75
00:02:40,156 --> 00:02:41,576
sixteen years old,

76
00:02:41,636 --> 00:02:44,095
a kid with a summer job and a pitcher's arm,

77
00:02:44,096 --> 00:02:45,163
just hired.

78
00:02:45,223 --> 00:02:47,142
Around nine o'clock, Bertha Tardy phoned

79
00:02:47,143 --> 00:02:50,022
a retired employee named Sam Jones and asked him

80
00:02:50,023 --> 00:02:52,429
to come in and help train the new hires.

81
00:02:52,489 --> 00:02:54,488
When Jones arrived about an hour later,

82
00:02:54,489 --> 00:02:56,128
he walked into the worst crime scene

83
00:02:56,129 --> 00:02:57,652
in the town's history.

84
00:02:57,712 --> 00:02:59,902
All four had been shot in the head.

85
00:02:59,962 --> 00:03:01,874
Three were already gone.

86
00:03:01,934 --> 00:03:04,542
Bobo Stewart, sixteen, was still breathing.

87
00:03:04,602 --> 00:03:06,001
He was rushed to the hospital,

88
00:03:06,002 --> 00:03:07,907
and he fought for six days.

89
00:03:07,967 --> 00:03:10,157
On July 22nd, he died too.

90
00:03:10,217 --> 00:03:11,479
Four people.

91
00:03:11,539 --> 00:03:14,245
A few hundred dollars missing from the drawer.

92
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:16,379
And no one left inside those walls to say

93
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:17,419
who did it.

94
00:03:17,479 --> 00:03:18,918
I want to be precise about what

95
00:03:18,919 --> 00:03:20,272
the killer left behind,

96
00:03:20,319 --> 00:03:23,025
because everything that follows depends on it.

97
00:03:23,073 --> 00:03:24,753
There was no murder weapon —

98
00:03:24,813 --> 00:03:26,564
none was ever found.

99
00:03:26,624 --> 00:03:28,861
There was no eyewitness to the crime.

100
00:03:28,921 --> 00:03:30,480
There was no fingerprint that answered

101
00:03:30,481 --> 00:03:33,759
the question, no camera, no confession.

102
00:03:33,819 --> 00:03:35,718
Understand what that empty room meant

103
00:03:35,719 --> 00:03:36,985
for the town.

104
00:03:37,045 --> 00:03:37,964
Winona is the kind

105
00:03:37,965 --> 00:03:39,824
of place where everyone is two handshakes

106
00:03:39,825 --> 00:03:41,245
from everyone else —

107
00:03:41,305 --> 00:03:42,344
where the victims' families

108
00:03:42,345 --> 00:03:43,864
and their neighbors passed each other

109
00:03:43,865 --> 00:03:44,984
at the grocery store

110
00:03:44,985 --> 00:03:46,824
and the church door every week,

111
00:03:46,825 --> 00:03:49,592
carrying the same unbearable question.

112
00:03:49,652 --> 00:03:53,232
A crime this terrible, and a case this empty —

113
00:03:53,292 --> 00:03:54,771
that combination is dangerous,

114
00:03:54,772 --> 00:03:57,051
because a town in agony demands a name,

115
00:03:57,052 --> 00:03:59,691
and it demands it fast.

116
00:03:59,751 --> 00:04:00,310
And the man

117
00:04:00,311 --> 00:04:03,270
whose job it was to provide one was the elected

118
00:04:03,271 --> 00:04:04,070
district attorney

119
00:04:04,071 --> 00:04:07,191
of Mississippi's Fifth Circuit Court District:

120
00:04:07,251 --> 00:04:08,391
Doug Evans —

121
00:04:08,451 --> 00:04:09,430
a white prosecutor,

122
00:04:09,431 --> 00:04:12,211
in office since before the murders,

123
00:04:12,271 --> 00:04:14,750
with a jurisdiction of small towns exactly like

124
00:04:14,751 --> 00:04:15,734
this one.

125
00:04:15,794 --> 00:04:17,799
Remember how thin the file is.

126
00:04:17,859 --> 00:04:19,957
Now watch a name get chosen.

127
00:04:20,017 --> 00:04:20,857
Within days,

128
00:04:20,917 --> 00:04:23,152
investigators fixed on Curtis Flowers.

129
00:04:23,196 --> 00:04:24,458
Why him?

130
00:04:24,518 --> 00:04:26,277
He had worked at Tardy Furniture —

131
00:04:26,278 --> 00:04:27,278
briefly —

132
00:04:27,338 --> 00:04:28,937
and had been let go about two weeks

133
00:04:28,938 --> 00:04:29,697
before the murders,

134
00:04:29,698 --> 00:04:32,357
in a dispute over roughly thirty dollars

135
00:04:32,358 --> 00:04:33,628
from his paycheck.

136
00:04:33,688 --> 00:04:35,187
That was the theory of the case,

137
00:04:35,188 --> 00:04:36,387
and I want you

138
00:04:36,388 --> 00:04:37,587
to hold it up to the light,

139
00:04:37,588 --> 00:04:40,727
because four death sentences will eventually rest

140
00:04:40,728 --> 00:04:41,628
on it:

141
00:04:41,688 --> 00:04:44,128
a man with no history of violence,

142
00:04:44,188 --> 00:04:46,388
no criminal record of any kind,

143
00:04:46,448 --> 00:04:48,167
a churchgoing gospel singer

144
00:04:48,168 --> 00:04:50,027
whose family was known across the county

145
00:04:50,028 --> 00:04:51,428
for their music —

146
00:04:51,488 --> 00:04:53,388
this man, the theory went,

147
00:04:53,448 --> 00:04:55,607
walked across town one July morning

148
00:04:55,608 --> 00:04:57,708
and executed four people,

149
00:04:57,768 --> 00:04:59,948
including a sixteen-year-old boy,

150
00:05:00,008 --> 00:05:03,324
over a lost part-time job and thirty dollars.

151
00:05:03,384 --> 00:05:04,423
From the first day,

152
00:05:04,424 --> 00:05:06,841
Flowers said he had nothing to do with it.

153
00:05:06,842 --> 00:05:09,081
He would say it, without a single deviation,

154
00:05:09,082 --> 00:05:11,215
for the next twenty-three years.

155
00:05:11,275 --> 00:05:12,908
But he was the name.

156
00:05:12,968 --> 00:05:14,548
And once he was the name,

157
00:05:14,608 --> 00:05:17,608
the case began to assemble itself around him —

158
00:05:17,668 --> 00:05:20,387
the way
a case does when the answer has been written first

159
00:05:20,388 --> 00:05:23,471
and the investigation is sent out to find it.

160
00:05:23,531 --> 00:05:25,990
Look at what the state built, piece by piece,

161
00:05:25,991 --> 00:05:26,910
because you are going

162
00:05:26,911 --> 00:05:29,550
to watch twelve strangers asked to kill a man

163
00:05:29,551 --> 00:05:31,248
on the strength of it.

164
00:05:31,308 --> 00:05:31,667
The gun:

165
00:05:31,668 --> 00:05:32,988
never found.

166
00:05:33,048 --> 00:05:34,927
Prosecutors said the bullets matched

167
00:05:34,928 --> 00:05:36,647
a pistol stolen that same morning

168
00:05:36,648 --> 00:05:37,887
from a parked car outside

169
00:05:37,888 --> 00:05:40,028
a garment plant across town —

170
00:05:40,088 --> 00:05:42,622
a gun that itself never turned up.

171
00:05:42,682 --> 00:05:43,281
The route:

172
00:05:43,282 --> 00:05:44,753
months after the murders,

173
00:05:44,782 --> 00:05:47,901
after reward money went public, witnesses began

174
00:05:47,902 --> 00:05:49,741
to surface saying they'd seen Flowers

175
00:05:49,742 --> 00:05:53,222
that morning, walking here, walking there,

176
00:05:53,282 --> 00:05:54,941
tracing a path from his home toward

177
00:05:54,942 --> 00:05:57,802
that parking lot and on toward the store —

178
00:05:57,862 --> 00:05:59,041
a paper trail of memory,

179
00:05:59,042 --> 00:06:00,621
assembled long after the fact,

180
00:06:00,622 --> 00:06:02,701
from a town that had heard for months

181
00:06:02,702 --> 00:06:04,331
who the suspect was.

182
00:06:04,391 --> 00:06:05,571
The science:

183
00:06:05,631 --> 00:06:07,970
a single microscopic particle on the back

184
00:06:07,971 --> 00:06:10,911
of Flowers' hand, swabbed hours later,

185
00:06:10,971 --> 00:06:12,950
of a kind experts will tell you can come

186
00:06:12,951 --> 00:06:14,391
from a gunshot —

187
00:06:14,451 --> 00:06:17,216
or from a police car, or a handcuff.

188
00:06:17,276 --> 00:06:18,955
And a bloody shoeprint at the scene

189
00:06:18,956 --> 00:06:20,816
in a common sneaker style,

190
00:06:20,876 --> 00:06:23,096
a size that fit Flowers and,

191
00:06:23,156 --> 00:06:24,980
as the defense would point out,

192
00:06:25,036 --> 00:06:27,036
an awful lot of other people.

193
00:06:27,096 --> 00:06:29,286
And the arrest split the town in two.

194
00:06:29,346 --> 00:06:30,786
In much of white Winona,

195
00:06:30,846 --> 00:06:32,745
the matter was settled the day the handcuffs went

196
00:06:32,746 --> 00:06:33,586
on:

197
00:06:33,646 --> 00:06:36,645
the DA had his man, and the town could exhale.

198
00:06:36,705 --> 00:06:38,165
In Black Winona —

199
00:06:38,225 --> 00:06:40,865
the churches where the Flowers family sang,

200
00:06:40,925 --> 00:06:43,184
the streets where Curtis had grown up in plain

201
00:06:43,185 --> 00:06:44,705
view of everyone —

202
00:06:44,765 --> 00:06:47,705
the reaction was closer to disbelief:

203
00:06:47,765 --> 00:06:49,484
they had known this man his whole life,

204
00:06:49,485 --> 00:06:52,317
and nothing in him matched the crime.

205
00:06:52,377 --> 00:06:54,077
Two towns, one case,

206
00:06:54,137 --> 00:06:56,336
and a line running between them that every jury

207
00:06:56,337 --> 00:06:58,515
in this story would be built along.

208
00:06:58,575 --> 00:06:59,375
That's it —

209
00:06:59,395 --> 00:07:01,648
because that really is the whole case.

210
00:07:01,708 --> 00:07:04,728
No weapon, no witness to the crime,

211
00:07:04,788 --> 00:07:07,467
no motive bigger than thirty dollars —

212
00:07:07,468 --> 00:07:10,627
a chain of maybes, each link soft,

213
00:07:10,628 --> 00:07:13,147
all of it pointing at a man with no record

214
00:07:13,148 --> 00:07:15,335
because it had been built to point at him.

215
00:07:15,336 --> 00:07:16,915
Any one of those links, alone,

216
00:07:16,916 --> 00:07:20,174
would embarrass a shoplifting prosecution.

217
00:07:20,234 --> 00:07:23,116
Braided together and told with enough confidence,

218
00:07:23,134 --> 00:07:25,633
they were about to be offered as proof beyond

219
00:07:25,634 --> 00:07:26,593
a reasonable doubt

220
00:07:26,594 --> 00:07:29,623
in the most serious charge American law has.

221
00:07:29,683 --> 00:07:31,463
In January 1997,

222
00:07:31,523 --> 00:07:33,362
Curtis Flowers was arrested for four counts

223
00:07:33,363 --> 00:07:34,903
of capital murder —

224
00:07:34,963 --> 00:07:37,737
the kind of murder that carries the death penalty.

225
00:07:37,738 --> 00:07:39,696
He was twenty-six years old.

226
00:07:39,756 --> 00:07:41,475
He would not take another free breath

227
00:07:41,476 --> 00:07:43,293
until he was forty-nine.

228
00:07:43,353 --> 00:07:44,893
A thin case, though,

229
00:07:44,953 --> 00:07:46,972
carries its own danger for the state —

230
00:07:46,973 --> 00:07:49,612
it is exactly the kind a fair jury can see

231
00:07:49,613 --> 00:07:50,513
through.

232
00:07:50,573 --> 00:07:52,949
Twelve ordinary people, sworn to doubt.

233
00:07:53,009 --> 00:07:53,928
That was the danger

234
00:07:53,929 --> 00:07:56,175
the state's case could not survive.

235
00:07:56,235 --> 00:07:57,194
Which means the story

236
00:07:57,195 --> 00:07:58,974
of the next twenty years is not really

237
00:07:58,975 --> 00:08:00,747
about evidence at all.

238
00:08:00,807 --> 00:08:02,926
It's about twelve chairs in a jury box —

239
00:08:02,927 --> 00:08:06,959
and who was allowed to sit in them.

240
00:08:07,019 --> 00:08:08,938
One rule before the count begins,

241
00:08:08,939 --> 00:08:11,160
because the whole film turns on it.

242
00:08:11,220 --> 00:08:12,440
In American courts,

243
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:14,119
each side can remove a certain number

244
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:16,847
of potential jurors without giving any reason.

245
00:08:16,907 --> 00:08:18,958
It's called a peremptory strike.

246
00:08:19,018 --> 00:08:20,518
And since 1986 —

247
00:08:20,578 --> 00:08:22,118
a case called Batson —

248
00:08:22,178 --> 00:08:24,517
the Constitution has said there is one reason you

249
00:08:24,518 --> 00:08:25,217
can never use:

250
00:08:25,218 --> 00:08:26,828
race.

251
00:08:26,888 --> 00:08:28,487
Strike a juror for being Black,

252
00:08:28,488 --> 00:08:30,472
and the trial is poisoned.

253
00:08:30,532 --> 00:08:31,979
Hold that rule.

254
00:08:32,039 --> 00:08:33,161
Now count.

255
00:08:33,221 --> 00:08:34,622
Trial one.

256
00:08:34,682 --> 00:08:36,872
October 1997.

257
00:08:36,932 --> 00:08:37,551
The publicity

258
00:08:37,552 --> 00:08:39,611
in Montgomery County was so saturating

259
00:08:39,612 --> 00:08:41,891
that the case was moved a hundred miles away,

260
00:08:41,892 --> 00:08:43,152
to Tupelo —

261
00:08:43,212 --> 00:08:45,552
and notice the first strategic choice:

262
00:08:45,612 --> 00:08:48,178
Flowers was not tried for the four murders.

263
00:08:48,238 --> 00:08:49,778
He was tried for one.

264
00:08:49,838 --> 00:08:51,598
Bertha Tardy's, alone —

265
00:08:51,658 --> 00:08:53,057
a smaller, cleaner story,

266
00:08:53,058 --> 00:08:55,497
with the other three counts held in reserve,

267
00:08:55,498 --> 00:08:58,902
so that even an acquittal would not end anything.

268
00:08:58,962 --> 00:09:00,362
Watch the jury:

269
00:09:00,422 --> 00:09:02,281
twelve seats, twelve white jurors,

270
00:09:02,282 --> 00:09:03,901
in a case pulled out of a county

271
00:09:03,902 --> 00:09:05,704
that is nearly half Black.

272
00:09:05,764 --> 00:09:08,064
The trial ran on the route witnesses,

273
00:09:08,124 --> 00:09:10,283
the borrowed ballistics, the particle —

274
00:09:10,284 --> 00:09:12,684
and it ran on the prosecutor's voice,

275
00:09:12,744 --> 00:09:14,283
filling the gaps in his evidence

276
00:09:14,284 --> 00:09:16,314
with the certainty of his telling.

277
00:09:16,374 --> 00:09:17,313
The jury convicted,

278
00:09:17,314 --> 00:09:19,633
and sentenced Curtis Flowers to death.

279
00:09:19,693 --> 00:09:20,815
That's one.

280
00:09:20,875 --> 00:09:23,110
He was sent to death row at Parchman —

281
00:09:23,155 --> 00:09:25,074
the Mississippi State Penitentiary,

282
00:09:25,075 --> 00:09:27,634
the most notorious prison farm in the South —

283
00:09:27,635 --> 00:09:28,994
to wait, at twenty-seven,

284
00:09:28,995 --> 00:09:31,843
for the state to set a date to kill him.

285
00:09:31,903 --> 00:09:33,622
And here is where this story leaves

286
00:09:33,623 --> 00:09:35,329
the road you think you're on.

287
00:09:35,361 --> 00:09:37,200
Because the conviction did not stand —

288
00:09:37,201 --> 00:09:41,360
and the reason it fell was not some technicality.

289
00:09:41,420 --> 00:09:44,340
In 2000, the Mississippi Supreme Court —

290
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:45,839
nine justices of one

291
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:48,680
of the most conservative courts in the South,

292
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:50,820
no friend of death-row appeals —

293
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:52,599
threw the verdict out because of what

294
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:56,160
the prosecutor himself had done in that courtroom:

295
00:09:56,220 --> 00:09:57,542
misconduct.

296
00:09:57,602 --> 00:10:00,301
He had argued facts that were never in evidence —

297
00:10:00,302 --> 00:10:02,321
told the jury things, again and again,

298
00:10:02,322 --> 00:10:05,365
that no witness had actually said from the stand.

299
00:10:05,425 --> 00:10:06,733
Read that again.

300
00:10:06,793 --> 00:10:08,132
The state's highest court looked

301
00:10:08,133 --> 00:10:10,152
at Doug Evans' first win and ruled

302
00:10:10,153 --> 00:10:12,234
that it had been taken unfairly.

303
00:10:12,294 --> 00:10:14,333
By then Curtis Flowers had spent three years

304
00:10:14,334 --> 00:10:16,393
on death row on the strength of it.

305
00:10:16,402 --> 00:10:18,778
And the remedy for those three years?

306
00:10:18,838 --> 00:10:20,146
Start over.

307
00:10:20,206 --> 00:10:21,746
The gap in the wall.

308
00:10:21,806 --> 00:10:22,966
A do-over —

309
00:10:23,026 --> 00:10:24,554
run by the same man.

310
00:10:24,614 --> 00:10:26,393
Except the do-over had already happened

311
00:10:26,394 --> 00:10:28,430
before the reversal came down.

312
00:10:28,490 --> 00:10:31,049
While the Tardy conviction was still on appeal,

313
00:10:31,050 --> 00:10:33,289
Evans had simply reached for the counts he'd kept

314
00:10:33,290 --> 00:10:34,535
in reserve.

315
00:10:34,595 --> 00:10:35,903
Trial two.

316
00:10:35,963 --> 00:10:39,103
March 1999, moved this time to the Gulf Coast,

317
00:10:39,163 --> 00:10:40,783
a different single count —

318
00:10:40,843 --> 00:10:43,494
the murder of sixteen-year-old Bobo Stewart.

319
00:10:43,554 --> 00:10:44,614
Watch the jury:

320
00:10:44,674 --> 00:10:46,905
eleven white jurors, one Black juror.

321
00:10:46,965 --> 00:10:48,134
Conviction.

322
00:10:48,194 --> 00:10:49,131
Death.

323
00:10:49,191 --> 00:10:50,453
That's two.

324
00:10:50,513 --> 00:10:53,532
And in 2003, the Mississippi Supreme Court threw

325
00:10:53,533 --> 00:10:55,333
that one out as well —

326
00:10:55,393 --> 00:10:57,951
prosecutorial misconduct, again.

327
00:10:58,011 --> 00:11:00,171
The same court, the same finding,

328
00:11:00,231 --> 00:11:01,966
the same name in the record.

329
00:11:02,026 --> 00:11:04,365
Twice now, Curtis Flowers had been strapped

330
00:11:04,366 --> 00:11:05,666
to a death sentence,

331
00:11:05,726 --> 00:11:08,245
and twice the highest court in the state had ruled

332
00:11:08,246 --> 00:11:10,985
that the man prosecuting him had crossed the line

333
00:11:10,986 --> 00:11:12,018
to get it.

334
00:11:12,078 --> 00:11:14,638
Add up what that cost the man in the cell:

335
00:11:14,698 --> 00:11:16,418
convicted in 1997,

336
00:11:16,478 --> 00:11:18,458
cleared of that verdict in 2000;

337
00:11:18,518 --> 00:11:22,395
convicted in 1999, cleared of that one in 2003.

338
00:11:22,455 --> 00:11:23,815
Six years gone,

339
00:11:23,875 --> 00:11:26,134
two death sentences served in the waiting room

340
00:11:26,135 --> 00:11:28,075
of the execution chamber —

341
00:11:28,135 --> 00:11:29,815
and in the eyes of the law,

342
00:11:29,875 --> 00:11:32,912
neither trial had ever validly happened at all.

343
00:11:32,972 --> 00:11:35,191
Most prosecutors never have one conviction

344
00:11:35,192 --> 00:11:38,181
reversed for misconduct in an entire career.

345
00:11:38,241 --> 00:11:39,980
Doug Evans was now two for two —

346
00:11:39,981 --> 00:11:42,614
and completely free to try again.

347
00:11:42,674 --> 00:11:45,333
Because here is the loophole inside the loophole:

348
00:11:45,334 --> 00:11:46,973
the court could erase his verdicts,

349
00:11:46,974 --> 00:11:49,382
but it could not remove him from the case.

350
00:11:49,383 --> 00:11:50,830
He was elected.

351
00:11:50,890 --> 00:11:52,802
He answered to no judge.

352
00:11:52,862 --> 00:11:54,901
Only the voters of his district could ever take

353
00:11:54,902 --> 00:11:56,562
the case away from him —

354
00:11:56,622 --> 00:11:58,561
and that fact is a loaded gun sitting

355
00:11:58,562 --> 00:12:00,347
on the table of this story.

356
00:12:00,407 --> 00:12:01,622
Leave it there.

357
00:12:01,682 --> 00:12:05,046
It goes off in the final act.

358
00:12:05,106 --> 00:12:06,309
Trial three.

359
00:12:06,369 --> 00:12:09,288
February 2004, back home in Winona,

360
00:12:09,289 --> 00:12:11,128
and now everything at once:

361
00:12:11,129 --> 00:12:13,900
all four murders, one trial.

362
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:15,200
Watch the jury —

363
00:12:15,260 --> 00:12:17,544
because this time, so did the court.

364
00:12:17,604 --> 00:12:20,491
Eleven white jurors, one Black juror, again.

365
00:12:20,551 --> 00:12:21,766
Conviction.

366
00:12:21,826 --> 00:12:22,855
Death.

367
00:12:22,915 --> 00:12:24,223
That's three.

368
00:12:24,283 --> 00:12:25,262
And when this one reached

369
00:12:25,263 --> 00:12:28,163
the Mississippi Supreme Court in 2007,

370
00:12:28,223 --> 00:12:30,467
the reversal changed species.

371
00:12:30,527 --> 00:12:33,089
Not misconduct in argument this time.

372
00:12:33,149 --> 00:12:35,468
The court found Doug Evans had used his strikes

373
00:12:35,469 --> 00:12:37,468
to remove Black citizens from the jury

374
00:12:37,469 --> 00:12:39,029
because they were Black —

375
00:12:39,089 --> 00:12:41,549
the exact thing Batson forbids —

376
00:12:41,609 --> 00:12:44,629
and it wrote a sentence courts almost never write:

377
00:12:44,689 --> 00:12:47,268
"The instant case presents us with as strong

378
00:12:47,269 --> 00:12:49,648
a prima facie case of racial discrimination

379
00:12:49,649 --> 00:12:51,908
as we have ever seen in the context

380
00:12:51,909 --> 00:12:53,637
of a Batson challenge."

381
00:12:53,697 --> 00:12:55,841
The strongest they had ever seen.

382
00:12:55,901 --> 00:12:58,835
That is not an activist's slogan.

383
00:12:58,895 --> 00:13:00,774
That is the Mississippi Supreme Court,

384
00:13:00,775 --> 00:13:02,834
describing the state's own prosecutor,

385
00:13:02,835 --> 00:13:05,219
in the state's own reports.

386
00:13:05,279 --> 00:13:06,541
Three trials.

387
00:13:06,601 --> 00:13:08,048
Three death sentences.

388
00:13:08,108 --> 00:13:09,628
Three reversals —

389
00:13:09,688 --> 00:13:12,029
and now the pattern had a name written on it.

390
00:13:12,030 --> 00:13:14,650
A machine was running in Montgomery County:

391
00:13:14,710 --> 00:13:17,190
choose the defendant, shape the jury,

392
00:13:17,250 --> 00:13:19,630
take the verdict, lose it on appeal,

393
00:13:19,690 --> 00:13:21,050
and start again —

394
00:13:21,110 --> 00:13:23,150
each trial funded by the public,

395
00:13:23,210 --> 00:13:25,569
each one grinding years off a man's life

396
00:13:25,570 --> 00:13:28,385
while he waited on death row at Parchman.

397
00:13:28,445 --> 00:13:30,724
The question you should be asking is the question

398
00:13:30,725 --> 00:13:33,104
a handful of people in Winona were starting

399
00:13:33,105 --> 00:13:34,805
to ask out loud:

400
00:13:34,865 --> 00:13:37,694
what happens if you just… keep going?

401
00:13:37,754 --> 00:13:40,033
How many times will the system let one man run

402
00:13:40,034 --> 00:13:41,291
the same play?

403
00:13:41,351 --> 00:13:43,050
The answer, it turned out, was:

404
00:13:43,051 --> 00:13:44,563
at least three more.

405
00:13:44,623 --> 00:13:46,682
But the next two trials would break the pattern

406
00:13:46,683 --> 00:13:48,823
in a way nobody expected —

407
00:13:48,883 --> 00:13:50,471
because for the first time,

408
00:13:50,523 --> 00:13:55,326
the jury box would not be almost entirely white.

409
00:13:55,386 --> 00:13:56,648
Trial four.

410
00:13:56,708 --> 00:13:58,295
2007.

411
00:13:58,355 --> 00:14:00,394
This time, five Black jurors made it

412
00:14:00,395 --> 00:14:03,055
into the box beside seven white jurors —

413
00:14:03,115 --> 00:14:04,374
the first jury in a decade

414
00:14:04,375 --> 00:14:06,493
that looked anything like the town —

415
00:14:06,495 --> 00:14:08,301
and everything changed.

416
00:14:08,361 --> 00:14:09,623
No verdict.

417
00:14:09,683 --> 00:14:12,322
The jury deadlocked, seven to five,

418
00:14:12,323 --> 00:14:14,262
and the split ran almost perfectly along

419
00:14:14,263 --> 00:14:15,622
the color line:

420
00:14:15,623 --> 00:14:17,882
seven white jurors voting guilty,

421
00:14:17,883 --> 00:14:20,000
five Black jurors voting to acquit.

422
00:14:20,060 --> 00:14:21,820
Look at that number honestly,

423
00:14:21,880 --> 00:14:23,439
because it cuts in every direction,

424
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:26,616
and this film is not going to flatten it.

425
00:14:26,676 --> 00:14:28,655
Twelve citizens of the same county heard

426
00:14:28,656 --> 00:14:31,476
the same witnesses, the same particle,

427
00:14:31,536 --> 00:14:33,216
the same borrowed bullets —

428
00:14:33,276 --> 00:14:35,395
and walked out unable to agree on what any

429
00:14:35,396 --> 00:14:36,668
of it proved.

430
00:14:36,728 --> 00:14:39,290
A hung jury is not an acquittal.

431
00:14:39,350 --> 00:14:42,010
But it is a verdict on the case itself:

432
00:14:42,070 --> 00:14:44,069
this evidence does not convince twelve fair

433
00:14:44,070 --> 00:14:45,116
people.

434
00:14:45,176 --> 00:14:46,484
It never had.

435
00:14:46,544 --> 00:14:47,163
The only thing

436
00:14:47,164 --> 00:14:49,543
that had ever produced unanimity was the removal,

437
00:14:49,544 --> 00:14:52,589
in advance, of the people most likely to doubt.

438
00:14:52,649 --> 00:14:53,911
Trial five.

439
00:14:53,971 --> 00:14:55,372
2008.

440
00:14:55,432 --> 00:14:57,808
Nine white jurors, three Black.

441
00:14:57,868 --> 00:14:59,288
Deadlocked again —

442
00:14:59,348 --> 00:15:01,928
that's two hung juries in two years —

443
00:15:01,988 --> 00:15:03,427
and this time the deadlock came down

444
00:15:03,428 --> 00:15:05,128
to a single holdout,

445
00:15:05,188 --> 00:15:07,328
one Black juror named James Bibbs,

446
00:15:07,388 --> 00:15:10,107
who was not persuaded a thin case had become

447
00:15:10,108 --> 00:15:11,343
a certain one.

448
00:15:11,403 --> 00:15:13,883
What happened next should stop you cold,

449
00:15:13,943 --> 00:15:15,202
because it is the moment

450
00:15:15,203 --> 00:15:17,355
this story shows you its teeth.

451
00:15:17,415 --> 00:15:19,954
When the mistrial was declared, James Bibbs —

452
00:15:19,955 --> 00:15:21,994
a juror, a citizen doing the duty

453
00:15:21,995 --> 00:15:24,015
the state had summoned him to do —

454
00:15:24,075 --> 00:15:25,934
was handcuffed in open court and charged

455
00:15:25,935 --> 00:15:27,129
with perjury.

456
00:15:27,189 --> 00:15:29,069
The prosecution pursuing him:

457
00:15:29,129 --> 00:15:30,215
Doug Evans.

458
00:15:30,275 --> 00:15:32,154
Think about what a charge like that says

459
00:15:32,155 --> 00:15:34,394
to every Black citizen of Montgomery County

460
00:15:34,395 --> 00:15:36,754
who might one day be called to sit

461
00:15:36,755 --> 00:15:38,275
in that box:

462
00:15:38,335 --> 00:15:39,254
vote the wrong way,

463
00:15:39,255 --> 00:15:41,774
and the man at the prosecution table may come

464
00:15:41,775 --> 00:15:43,007
for you next.

465
00:15:43,067 --> 00:15:47,267
The state attorney general's office eventually
stepped in, took the case away,

466
00:15:47,327 --> 00:15:49,251
and dropped the charge entirely.

467
00:15:49,311 --> 00:15:51,790
But the message had been broadcast, in handcuffs,

468
00:15:51,791 --> 00:15:53,070
in a public courtroom —

469
00:15:53,071 --> 00:15:55,867
and it did not need repeating.

470
00:15:55,927 --> 00:15:57,374
Trial six.

471
00:15:57,434 --> 00:15:58,881
June 2010.

472
00:15:58,941 --> 00:16:00,017
Winona.

473
00:16:00,077 --> 00:16:02,778
Fourteen years had passed since the murders.

474
00:16:02,838 --> 00:16:05,758
The route witnesses were fourteen years older;

475
00:16:05,818 --> 00:16:08,837
the case had aged, but it had not improved.

476
00:16:08,897 --> 00:16:10,977
Watch the jury one last time:

477
00:16:11,037 --> 00:16:13,977
eleven white jurors, one Black juror —

478
00:16:14,037 --> 00:16:15,216
the arithmetic restored

479
00:16:15,217 --> 00:16:17,116
to what it had been whenever the state had won

480
00:16:17,117 --> 00:16:18,146
before.

481
00:16:18,206 --> 00:16:20,585
The deliberations reportedly took around half

482
00:16:20,586 --> 00:16:21,604
an hour.

483
00:16:21,664 --> 00:16:22,423
Half an hour,

484
00:16:22,424 --> 00:16:24,763
for four capital counts and fourteen years

485
00:16:24,764 --> 00:16:26,409
of contested evidence.

486
00:16:26,469 --> 00:16:28,195
Guilty on all four.

487
00:16:28,255 --> 00:16:29,284
Death.

488
00:16:29,344 --> 00:16:32,109
That's six trials, and a fourth death sentence,

489
00:16:32,164 --> 00:16:33,183
and Curtis Flowers —

490
00:16:33,184 --> 00:16:34,704
forty years old now —

491
00:16:34,764 --> 00:16:36,123
went back to the row at Parchman,

492
00:16:36,124 --> 00:16:39,151
where he had already spent most of his adult life.

493
00:16:39,211 --> 00:16:41,930
As far as the State of Mississippi was concerned,

494
00:16:41,931 --> 00:16:43,890
the machine had finally produced a verdict

495
00:16:43,891 --> 00:16:45,256
that would stick.

496
00:16:45,316 --> 00:16:47,971
And for eight years, it did stick.

497
00:16:48,031 --> 00:16:50,796
The appeal from trial six ground its way upward

498
00:16:50,811 --> 00:16:53,458
while Curtis Flowers passed his mid-thirties,

499
00:16:53,511 --> 00:16:56,071
then his forties, in a death-row cell —

500
00:16:56,131 --> 00:16:58,170
a man now old enough to have spent more

501
00:16:58,171 --> 00:17:01,042
of his life inside the case than outside it.

502
00:17:01,102 --> 00:17:02,689
The town moved on.

503
00:17:02,749 --> 00:17:04,243
The store changed.

504
00:17:04,303 --> 00:17:05,342
The machine, at last,

505
00:17:05,343 --> 00:17:08,072
seemed to have won by outlasting everyone.

506
00:17:08,132 --> 00:17:11,771
It would have kept winning —
if the case's most important witness

507
00:17:11,772 --> 00:17:13,713
had been telling the truth.

508
00:17:13,773 --> 00:17:15,452
Because there is a character I have kept

509
00:17:15,453 --> 00:17:16,893
off camera until now,

510
00:17:16,953 --> 00:17:19,864
and he is the floor this whole building stood on.

511
00:17:19,924 --> 00:17:21,789
His name was Odell Hallmon.

512
00:17:21,849 --> 00:17:23,668
They called him Cookie.

513
00:17:23,728 --> 00:17:26,127
A career criminal from the same county,

514
00:17:26,128 --> 00:17:28,422
in and out of Parchman his entire life.

515
00:17:28,440 --> 00:17:30,119
Beginning with the second trial,

516
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:33,280
Hallmon gave the state
the one thing its case never had:

517
00:17:33,340 --> 00:17:34,857
direct evidence.

518
00:17:34,917 --> 00:17:36,976
He swore that Curtis Flowers had confessed

519
00:17:36,977 --> 00:17:38,097
to him —

520
00:17:38,157 --> 00:17:41,334
cellblock to cellblock, killer to confidant.

521
00:17:41,394 --> 00:17:43,293
He told that story on the stand in four

522
00:17:43,294 --> 00:17:44,699
of the six trials.

523
00:17:44,759 --> 00:17:46,918
When jurors later explained their guilty votes

524
00:17:46,919 --> 00:17:48,319
to reporters,

525
00:17:48,379 --> 00:17:50,458
that jailhouse confession was the piece they

526
00:17:50,459 --> 00:17:51,547
reached for.

527
00:17:51,607 --> 00:17:52,962
Not the particle.

528
00:17:53,022 --> 00:17:54,237
Not the route.

529
00:17:54,297 --> 00:17:55,326
Him.

530
00:17:55,386 --> 00:17:57,251
So who was Odell Hallmon?

531
00:17:57,311 --> 00:17:59,530
Follow his file through the same years,

532
00:17:59,531 --> 00:18:00,890
and you find a man

533
00:18:00,891 --> 00:18:03,270
whose own serious charges kept resolving

534
00:18:03,271 --> 00:18:04,750
with remarkable gentleness

535
00:18:04,751 --> 00:18:07,451
while he remained useful to the state —

536
00:18:07,511 --> 00:18:09,310
a pattern documented across years

537
00:18:09,311 --> 00:18:10,833
of court records.

538
00:18:10,893 --> 00:18:14,373
Drug charges, gun charges, violence —

539
00:18:14,433 --> 00:18:15,492
the file kept growing,

540
00:18:15,493 --> 00:18:18,517
and the consequences kept not arriving.

541
00:18:18,577 --> 00:18:20,876
Ask yourself what a jailhouse confession is worth

542
00:18:20,877 --> 00:18:23,415
when the man selling it is running a tab.

543
00:18:23,475 --> 00:18:25,694
Four separate juries were never really given

544
00:18:25,695 --> 00:18:27,523
the chance to do that math.

545
00:18:27,583 --> 00:18:29,323
And then, in 2016,

546
00:18:29,383 --> 00:18:31,682
the state's star witness showed the world exactly

547
00:18:31,683 --> 00:18:32,978
who he was.

548
00:18:33,038 --> 00:18:34,617
Odell Hallmon took a gun

549
00:18:34,618 --> 00:18:36,358
and murdered three people —

550
00:18:36,418 --> 00:18:38,738
his ex-girlfriend, Marquita Hill;

551
00:18:38,798 --> 00:18:40,818
her mother, Carolyn Ann Sanders;

552
00:18:40,878 --> 00:18:42,797
and a man named Kenneth Loggins —

553
00:18:42,798 --> 00:18:44,284
and wounded a fourth.

554
00:18:44,344 --> 00:18:46,952
He pleaded guilty about two weeks later.

555
00:18:47,012 --> 00:18:48,320
Three murders.

556
00:18:48,380 --> 00:18:49,735
Life in prison.

557
00:18:49,795 --> 00:18:51,055
Sit with this:

558
00:18:51,115 --> 00:18:52,934
the witness whose word put Curtis Flowers

559
00:18:52,935 --> 00:18:55,134
on death row four times went on to commit

560
00:18:55,135 --> 00:18:58,034
the very kind of crime Flowers was condemned for —

561
00:18:58,035 --> 00:18:59,874
while Flowers, who had never hurt anyone

562
00:18:59,875 --> 00:19:01,915
on any record ever produced,

563
00:19:01,975 --> 00:19:05,035
sat in a cell measuring his life in appeals.

564
00:19:05,095 --> 00:19:07,703
And the floor had one more board to give.

565
00:19:07,763 --> 00:19:08,983
In 2018,

566
00:19:09,043 --> 00:19:11,522
investigative reporters who had spent a year
in Winona pulling

567
00:19:11,523 --> 00:19:13,902
this case apart got Odell Hallmon

568
00:19:13,903 --> 00:19:16,084
on a recorded line from prison.

569
00:19:16,144 --> 00:19:19,124
And on tape, the state's star witness said this:

570
00:19:19,184 --> 00:19:21,363
"As far as him telling me he killed some people,

571
00:19:21,364 --> 00:19:24,465
hell, naw, he ain't never told me that.

572
00:19:24,525 --> 00:19:25,926
That was a lie."

573
00:19:25,986 --> 00:19:27,387
That was a lie.

574
00:19:27,447 --> 00:19:28,802
Four trials.

575
00:19:28,862 --> 00:19:31,282
Four juries told about a confession that,

576
00:19:31,342 --> 00:19:33,341
by the teller's own recorded words,

577
00:19:33,342 --> 00:19:34,396
never happened.

578
00:19:34,456 --> 00:19:36,715
The only direct evidence in six prosecutions —

579
00:19:36,716 --> 00:19:38,922
gone, in one sentence.

580
00:19:38,982 --> 00:19:40,821
The tower was standing on nothing,

581
00:19:40,822 --> 00:19:43,262
and now the nothing had said so out loud.

582
00:19:43,322 --> 00:19:44,761
The question was whether anyone

583
00:19:44,762 --> 00:19:46,441
with power would listen.

584
00:19:46,501 --> 00:19:49,060
The answer came from two directions at once —

585
00:19:49,061 --> 00:19:49,740
a microphone,

586
00:19:49,741 --> 00:19:54,232
and the Supreme Court of the United States.

587
00:19:54,292 --> 00:19:57,212
The reporters were from American Public Media —

588
00:19:57,272 --> 00:19:59,251
a team led by Madeleine Baran,

589
00:19:59,252 --> 00:20:01,870
and a podcast called In the Dark.

590
00:20:01,930 --> 00:20:04,709
In 2018 they did what six trials somehow never

591
00:20:04,710 --> 00:20:05,489
had:

592
00:20:05,490 --> 00:20:06,907
they checked everything.

593
00:20:06,967 --> 00:20:08,346
They re-walked the route and found

594
00:20:08,347 --> 00:20:11,053
the state's timeline of sightings dissolving —

595
00:20:11,107 --> 00:20:12,666
key route witnesses recanted

596
00:20:12,667 --> 00:20:15,288
or contradicted themselves on the record.

597
00:20:15,348 --> 00:20:16,427
They took the ballistics

598
00:20:16,428 --> 00:20:18,428
to independent firearms examiners,

599
00:20:18,448 --> 00:20:20,487
who would not stand behind the certainty

600
00:20:20,488 --> 00:20:22,275
a jury had been sold.

601
00:20:22,335 --> 00:20:25,276
They asked what a single particle on a man's hand,

602
00:20:25,315 --> 00:20:28,075
swabbed hours later, actually proves,

603
00:20:28,135 --> 00:20:29,775
and the answer was:

604
00:20:29,835 --> 00:20:32,002
nothing a life should hang on.

605
00:20:32,062 --> 00:20:33,001
Piece by piece,

606
00:20:33,002 --> 00:20:34,881
the case that had consumed twenty years

607
00:20:34,882 --> 00:20:37,541
and four death sentences came apart on tape,

608
00:20:37,542 --> 00:20:40,615
in public, for millions of listeners.

609
00:20:40,675 --> 00:20:43,794
But their most devastating work was arithmetic.

610
00:20:43,854 --> 00:20:45,033
Jury selection records

611
00:20:45,034 --> 00:20:46,793
in rural Mississippi are not sitting

612
00:20:46,794 --> 00:20:48,114
in a database;

613
00:20:48,174 --> 00:20:50,967
they are ink on paper in courthouse storerooms.

614
00:20:51,027 --> 00:20:52,987
The reporters went and got them —

615
00:20:53,047 --> 00:20:55,247
county by county, trial by trial,

616
00:20:55,307 --> 00:20:57,547
across Evans' entire district —

617
00:20:57,607 --> 00:20:59,166
and built the dataset nobody

618
00:20:59,167 --> 00:21:01,307
in power had ever bothered to build:

619
00:21:01,367 --> 00:21:07,306
more than six thousand seven hundred jurors,
two hundred twenty-five trials,

620
00:21:07,307 --> 00:21:08,943
a quarter-century of records.
The finding:

621
00:21:08,944 --> 00:21:11,204
across his tenure,

622
00:21:11,264 --> 00:21:13,583
Doug Evans' office struck Black prospective jurors

623
00:21:13,584 --> 00:21:15,083
at nearly four and a half times

624
00:21:15,084 --> 00:21:16,849
the rate it struck white ones.

625
00:21:16,890 --> 00:21:19,731
Half of all eligible Black jurors, struck.

626
00:21:19,791 --> 00:21:21,981
Eleven percent of white ones.

627
00:21:22,041 --> 00:21:24,040
And the disparity held even when Black

628
00:21:24,041 --> 00:21:25,880
and white jurors gave the same answers

629
00:21:25,881 --> 00:21:27,482
to the same questions.

630
00:21:27,542 --> 00:21:29,129
Not one case.

631
00:21:29,189 --> 00:21:31,240
Not one bad afternoon.

632
00:21:31,300 --> 00:21:34,620
A pattern, running the length of a career —

633
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:35,479
the same pattern

634
00:21:35,480 --> 00:21:37,279
the Mississippi Supreme Court had called

635
00:21:37,280 --> 00:21:39,260
the strongest it had ever seen,

636
00:21:39,320 --> 00:21:41,019
now measured across everything

637
00:21:41,020 --> 00:21:42,732
the man had ever touched.

638
00:21:42,792 --> 00:21:44,471
And that is what was sitting in the open

639
00:21:44,472 --> 00:21:46,851
when Flowers' appeal from trial six reached

640
00:21:46,852 --> 00:21:48,976
the United States Supreme Court.

641
00:21:49,036 --> 00:21:50,994
June 21st, 2019.

642
00:21:51,054 --> 00:21:53,105
Flowers versus Mississippi.

643
00:21:53,165 --> 00:21:54,804
Seven justices to two,

644
00:21:54,805 --> 00:21:57,845
in an opinion by Justice Brett Kavanaugh —

645
00:21:57,905 --> 00:22:00,204
a Trump appointee, writing for justices across

646
00:22:00,205 --> 00:22:03,105
the Court's entire ideological spectrum —

647
00:22:03,165 --> 00:22:05,492
the Court threw out the sixth conviction.

648
00:22:05,493 --> 00:22:07,637
And it did not do it quietly.

649
00:22:07,697 --> 00:22:09,896
The opinion walks through Doug Evans' six trials

650
00:22:09,897 --> 00:22:11,796
by name, and then it does what

651
00:22:11,797 --> 00:22:14,160
this film has been doing all along.

652
00:22:14,220 --> 00:22:15,435
It counts.

653
00:22:15,495 --> 00:22:18,275
In the six trials combined, the Court wrote,

654
00:22:18,335 --> 00:22:20,534
the state used its strikes against "41

655
00:22:20,535 --> 00:22:22,734
of the 42 black prospective jurors

656
00:22:22,735 --> 00:22:24,837
that it could have struck.

657
00:22:24,897 --> 00:22:26,837
In the first four trials,

658
00:22:26,897 --> 00:22:29,597
thirty-six Black prospective jurors came up —

659
00:22:29,657 --> 00:22:32,196
"The State tried to strike all 36.

660
00:22:32,256 --> 00:22:33,436
In trial six,

661
00:22:33,496 --> 00:22:35,735
the state struck five of the six Black citizens

662
00:22:35,736 --> 00:22:38,135
in the pool, and the Court noticed how:

663
00:22:38,136 --> 00:22:41,255
it had asked those five Black prospective jurors

664
00:22:41,256 --> 00:22:44,375
a hundred and forty-five questions —

665
00:22:44,376 --> 00:22:46,215
hunting for any excuse —

666
00:22:46,216 --> 00:22:48,075
while asking the eleven seated white jurors

667
00:22:48,076 --> 00:22:49,214
a total of twelve.

668
00:22:49,274 --> 00:22:52,115
"The numbers speak loudly," Kavanaugh wrote.

669
00:22:52,175 --> 00:22:53,114
And then the sentence

670
00:22:53,115 --> 00:22:55,335
that will follow Doug Evans forever:

671
00:22:55,395 --> 00:22:56,454
the state's "relentless,

672
00:22:56,455 --> 00:22:58,594
determined effort to rid the jury

673
00:22:58,595 --> 00:23:01,355
of black individuals," the Court wrote,

674
00:23:01,415 --> 00:23:02,854
"strongly suggests" it wanted

675
00:23:02,855 --> 00:23:06,486
to try Flowers "ideally before an all-white jury.

676
00:23:06,546 --> 00:23:09,866
Equal justice under law, the Court said,

677
00:23:09,926 --> 00:23:11,985
"requires a criminal trial free

678
00:23:11,986 --> 00:23:13,125
of racial discrimination

679
00:23:13,126 --> 00:23:15,145
in the jury selection process.

680
00:23:15,205 --> 00:23:17,065
Two justices dissented,

681
00:23:17,125 --> 00:23:19,784
arguing the strikes had race-neutral explanations

682
00:23:19,785 --> 00:23:22,245
the majority was steamrolling —

683
00:23:22,305 --> 00:23:24,524
and were answered by the opinion's plain refusal

684
00:23:24,525 --> 00:23:27,165
to look at trial six in a vacuum:

685
00:23:27,225 --> 00:23:29,685
you cannot understand strike forty-two,

686
00:23:29,745 --> 00:23:31,039
the majority reasoned,

687
00:23:31,085 --> 00:23:33,476
without the forty-one that came before it.

688
00:23:33,477 --> 00:23:34,916
The count had made it all the way

689
00:23:34,917 --> 00:23:36,937
to the top of American law:

690
00:23:36,997 --> 00:23:39,150
forty-one out of forty-two.

691
00:23:39,210 --> 00:23:41,168
The sixth conviction was gone.

692
00:23:41,228 --> 00:23:42,428
And this time,

693
00:23:42,488 --> 00:23:44,247
the machine could not simply reset —

694
00:23:44,248 --> 00:23:47,552
because now the whole country was watching it try.

695
00:23:47,612 --> 00:23:49,663
December 16th, 2019.

696
00:23:49,723 --> 00:23:51,343
A Mississippi courtroom,

697
00:23:51,403 --> 00:23:53,342
the same circuit judge who had presided

698
00:23:53,343 --> 00:23:55,103
over the later trials —

699
00:23:55,163 --> 00:23:57,722
and for the first time in twenty-three years,

700
00:23:57,723 --> 00:23:58,601
bail.

701
00:23:58,661 --> 00:24:02,221
Two hundred fifty thousand dollars,

702
00:24:02,281 --> 00:24:03,040
an ankle monitor,

703
00:24:03,041 --> 00:24:04,760
and Curtis Flowers walked out of jail

704
00:24:04,761 --> 00:24:06,920
into the December air a forty-nine-year-old man

705
00:24:06,921 --> 00:24:09,177
who had entered the system at twenty-six.

706
00:24:09,178 --> 00:24:11,958
Every step of his adult life to that point —

707
00:24:12,018 --> 00:24:14,957
every appeal, every reversal, every retrial —

708
00:24:14,958 --> 00:24:17,173
had happened in custody.

709
00:24:17,233 --> 00:24:19,412
This was the first door in twenty-three years

710
00:24:19,413 --> 00:24:20,863
that opened outward.

711
00:24:20,923 --> 00:24:22,683
Weeks later, Doug Evans —

712
00:24:22,743 --> 00:24:24,562
the prosecutor of all six trials —

713
00:24:24,563 --> 00:24:28,036
finally let go of the case, recusing himself.

714
00:24:28,096 --> 00:24:28,415
It went

715
00:24:28,416 --> 00:24:30,995
to the Mississippi Attorney General's office,

716
00:24:30,996 --> 00:24:32,215
which did what a fresh set

717
00:24:32,216 --> 00:24:34,187
of eyes was always going to do.

718
00:24:34,247 --> 00:24:36,147
On September 4th, 2020,

719
00:24:36,207 --> 00:24:38,147
the state dropped every charge —

720
00:24:38,207 --> 00:24:39,907
dismissed with prejudice,

721
00:24:39,967 --> 00:24:42,057
the legal words for never again.

722
00:24:42,117 --> 00:24:43,657
No seventh trial.

723
00:24:43,717 --> 00:24:44,886
Not ever.

724
00:24:44,946 --> 00:24:46,965
After six trials, four death sentences,

725
00:24:46,966 --> 00:24:48,386
and twenty-three years,

726
00:24:48,446 --> 00:24:50,505
the State of Mississippi walked away from the case

727
00:24:50,506 --> 00:24:52,305
against Curtis Flowers without a conviction

728
00:24:52,306 --> 00:24:53,545
to its name.

729
00:24:53,605 --> 00:24:56,028
What does a state owe a man for that?

730
00:24:56,088 --> 00:24:57,687
Mississippi's answer is written

731
00:24:57,688 --> 00:24:59,448
in its own statute:

732
00:24:59,508 --> 00:25:03,114
fifty thousand dollars per lost year —

733
00:25:03,174 --> 00:25:06,426
capped at ten years.
Curtis Flowers lost nearly twenty-three.

734
00:25:06,486 --> 00:25:09,285
In March 2021 a judge awarded him the maximum,

735
00:25:09,286 --> 00:25:11,905
five hundred thousand dollars,

736
00:25:11,906 --> 00:25:14,565
paid in yearly installments —

737
00:25:14,566 --> 00:25:15,685
compensation, by law,

738
00:25:15,686 --> 00:25:17,884
for less than half the life that was taken.

739
00:25:17,885 --> 00:25:19,904
He sued Doug Evans in federal court;

740
00:25:19,905 --> 00:25:23,001
the case settled in 2023, terms undisclosed.

741
00:25:23,061 --> 00:25:24,648
And Evans himself?

742
00:25:24,708 --> 00:25:25,787
Understand this clearly,

743
00:25:25,788 --> 00:25:28,047
because this film will not lie to you to make

744
00:25:28,048 --> 00:25:29,528
the ending feel better:

745
00:25:29,588 --> 00:25:31,682
he was never charged with anything.

746
00:25:31,742 --> 00:25:33,561
Never disciplined by the bar.

747
00:25:33,621 --> 00:25:35,765
Prosecutorial immunity held.

748
00:25:35,825 --> 00:25:37,684
The man whose conduct four reversals

749
00:25:37,685 --> 00:25:39,804
and the Supreme Court of the United States wrote

750
00:25:39,805 --> 00:25:42,725
into the permanent record kept his office,

751
00:25:42,785 --> 00:25:44,703
his license, and his pension.

752
00:25:44,763 --> 00:25:46,663
But the record was now public —

753
00:25:46,723 --> 00:25:49,415
and the people of his district had heard it.

754
00:25:49,475 --> 00:25:51,835
Remember the loaded gun from act two:

755
00:25:51,895 --> 00:25:53,634
only the voters could take the cases away

756
00:25:53,635 --> 00:25:54,684
from him.

757
00:25:54,744 --> 00:25:56,604
In November 2022,

758
00:25:56,664 --> 00:25:59,504
Doug Evans asked those voters to elevate him —

759
00:25:59,564 --> 00:26:02,263
to make him a circuit judge, a robe above

760
00:26:02,264 --> 00:26:05,015
the courtroom where all six trials ran.

761
00:26:05,075 --> 00:26:06,474
The district that had employed him

762
00:26:06,475 --> 00:26:08,795
for three decades gave its answer:

763
00:26:08,855 --> 00:26:11,770
seventy percent to thirty, for his opponent.

764
00:26:11,830 --> 00:26:12,549
A landslide,

765
00:26:12,550 --> 00:26:14,856
against the man the numbers had caught.

766
00:26:14,916 --> 00:26:16,435
Seven months later he retired

767
00:26:16,436 --> 00:26:19,568
as district attorney, gone before his term was up.

768
00:26:19,628 --> 00:26:21,911
And the reckoning has not closed.

769
00:26:21,971 --> 00:26:23,751
In March 2025,

770
00:26:23,811 --> 00:26:25,950
a veteran attorney-discipline official petitioned

771
00:26:25,951 --> 00:26:27,811
the Mississippi Supreme Court —

772
00:26:27,871 --> 00:26:30,691
the same court that reversed him three times —

773
00:26:30,751 --> 00:26:34,146
to suspend Doug Evans' license to practice law.

774
00:26:34,206 --> 00:26:36,907
As of this film, that question is still open.

775
00:26:36,967 --> 00:26:40,936
The record he wrote is still following him.

776
00:26:40,996 --> 00:26:42,676
So run the final count.

777
00:26:42,736 --> 00:26:43,951
Six trials.

778
00:26:44,011 --> 00:26:45,250
Four death sentences —

779
00:26:45,251 --> 00:26:46,619
all four erased.

780
00:26:46,679 --> 00:26:48,080
Two hung juries.

781
00:26:48,140 --> 00:26:50,459
One prosecutor, whose name is now fixed

782
00:26:50,460 --> 00:26:52,379
in a Supreme Court opinion beside

783
00:26:52,380 --> 00:26:54,649
the words relentless and determined.

784
00:26:54,709 --> 00:26:57,248
Twenty-three years of one innocent man's life,

785
00:26:57,249 --> 00:27:00,429
traded for a five-hundred-thousand-dollar cap.

786
00:27:00,489 --> 00:27:03,128
Zero criminal consequences for the man who ran

787
00:27:03,129 --> 00:27:04,584
the prosecution.

788
00:27:04,644 --> 00:27:06,173
That is the whole ledger —

789
00:27:06,224 --> 00:27:08,084
except for the last line,

790
00:27:08,144 --> 00:27:09,523
and the last line is the one

791
00:27:09,524 --> 00:27:12,129
this story will not let you look away from.

792
00:27:12,189 --> 00:27:14,428
Nobody was ever held accountable for the murders

793
00:27:14,429 --> 00:27:18,429
of Bertha Tardy, Carmen Rigby, Robert Golden,

794
00:27:18,489 --> 00:27:20,881
and sixteen-year-old Derrick Stewart.

795
00:27:20,941 --> 00:27:23,224
The case is officially unsolved.

796
00:27:23,284 --> 00:27:26,443
Whoever walked into Tardy Furniture on July 16th,

797
00:27:26,444 --> 00:27:29,284
1996 has never answered for it —

798
00:27:29,344 --> 00:27:30,863
because for twenty-three years,

799
00:27:30,864 --> 00:27:33,843
the full weight of the State
of Mississippi was aimed at proving

800
00:27:33,844 --> 00:27:36,109
a theory instead of finding the truth.

801
00:27:36,169 --> 00:27:38,028
Six trials didn't just nearly kill

802
00:27:38,029 --> 00:27:39,474
an innocent man.

803
00:27:39,534 --> 00:27:41,293
They spent the only investigation

804
00:27:41,294 --> 00:27:43,582
those four families will ever get.

805
00:27:43,642 --> 00:27:45,507
Curtis Flowers is free today.

806
00:27:45,567 --> 00:27:46,922
He sings again.

807
00:27:46,982 --> 00:27:48,481
He got his life back at forty-nine —

808
00:27:48,482 --> 00:27:50,221
and what the record shows,

809
00:27:50,222 --> 00:27:53,821
at the end of all the counting,
is that he is the only man who ever paid

810
00:27:53,822 --> 00:27:55,441
for what happened at Tardy Furniture,

811
00:27:55,442 --> 00:27:58,042
and the only one who owed nothing.

812
00:27:58,102 --> 00:28:00,525
One more number, and I'll let you go.

813
00:28:00,585 --> 00:28:02,984
Fifty percent of eligible Black jurors struck,

814
00:28:02,985 --> 00:28:05,204
against eleven percent of white ones —

815
00:28:05,205 --> 00:28:08,464
for a quarter of a century, in one small district,

816
00:28:08,465 --> 00:28:10,184
before anyone with a microphone

817
00:28:10,185 --> 00:28:12,017
or a gavel added it up.

818
00:28:12,077 --> 00:28:13,376
Winona is not the only town

819
00:28:13,377 --> 00:28:15,116
with courthouse storerooms full of paper

820
00:28:15,117 --> 00:28:17,054
that no one has counted.

821
00:28:17,114 --> 00:28:19,351
That is not a Mississippi story.

822
00:28:19,411 --> 00:28:21,890
That is a math problem sitting, right now,

823
00:28:21,891 --> 00:28:23,970
in every state in the country.

824
00:28:24,030 --> 00:28:25,009
If you believe a jury

825
00:28:25,010 --> 00:28:27,329
of your peers should mean all of your peers —

826
00:28:27,330 --> 00:28:30,754
hit like, so the next count comes out different.
